# .env.example — template for local environment variables.
#
# What this file owns:
#   The authoritative list of every environment variable this application reads. It is
#   committed to git on purpose and must contain ONLY placeholder values — never a real
#   credential. `.gitignore` excludes `.env*` but explicitly re-includes this file.
#
# How to use it:
#   cp .env.example .env.local
#   then fill in real values. `.env.local` is git-ignored and is what Next.js loads in
#   development. Production values are set in the hosting provider's dashboard, per
#   environment — preview and production are configured separately and do not share keys.
#
# Rule: adding a new `process.env.X` read anywhere in the codebase requires adding X here
# in the same change, with a comment describing what it is and where to obtain it.
#
# Current state: no variables are required.
#   The site is entirely static content — no database, no CMS, no auth, no secrets. If a
#   contact form endpoint is added later, its email-provider key and destination address
#   belong here, with safe placeholders.
